WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.932 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.932 --> 00:00:03.381 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.381 --> 00:00:05.796 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.796 --> 00:00:11.420 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.640 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: So today we're
going to cover an introduction

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.640 align:middle line:90%
to time series analysis.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.920 align:middle line:84%
And time series
analysis is really

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:24.880 align:middle line:84%
a very powerful
methodology in statistics,

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.720 align:middle line:84%
and especially powerful
when it comes to modeling

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:29.980 align:middle line:90%
financial markets.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.680 align:middle line:84%
So I hope you'll find
this introduction

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.320 align:middle line:90%
engaging and motivating.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.360 align:middle line:90%
So let's talk about time series.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.920 align:middle line:84%
We basically have notation
indicating a stochastic process

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.680 align:middle line:84%
where X corresponds to the
value of the process at time T,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:59.790 align:middle line:84%
and the time periods
T can be discrete.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.110 align:middle line:84%
This is most commonly
what we'll work with.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:09.670 align:middle line:84%
But it can also correspond to
a continuous time interval.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:16.630 align:middle line:84%
And so we can imagine basically
time periods, time indexed

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.910 align:middle line:90%
by positive numbers from zero.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:26.950 align:middle line:84%
And in terms of modeling
such a time series,

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.830 align:middle line:84%
we want to be able to
specify the probability

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:38.510 align:middle line:84%
model for any collection
of time point values.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:43.470 align:middle line:84%
So when we say, let's specify
a probability model for a time

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.630 align:middle line:84%
series, then that
corresponds to being

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:52.530 align:middle line:84%
able to specify the joint
density of any set of points--

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.750 align:middle line:84%
here we have m
different time points.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:59.270 align:middle line:84%
This generalizes
to all cases of m.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:04.790 align:middle line:84%
And such a subset of the
values of the process

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:10.710 align:middle line:84%
are a finite dimensional
set of values,

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.630 align:middle line:84%
and so we'll have finite
dimensional distributions

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.830 align:middle line:90%
for xt.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:23.670 align:middle line:84%
Now importantly, we'll want
to consider time series that

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.990 align:middle line:90%
are strictly stationary.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.110 align:middle line:84%
And a strictly
stationary time series

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:35.590 align:middle line:84%
is one for which the
distribution of any set of time

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:43.510 align:middle line:84%
points is equal to the
distribution at the same time

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:46.310 align:middle line:90%
points shifted by a constant.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:52.150 align:middle line:84%
So if we're looking
at the index of time

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:58.420 align:middle line:84%
and we have xt, if we look
at, say, four points here,

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:03.940 align:middle line:84%
T1, T2, T3, T4, then
the distribution

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:08.780 align:middle line:84%
of the values at
these time points

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.340 align:middle line:84%
will be the same
as the distribution

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:16.780 align:middle line:84%
if we shift these time points
all by the same amount tau.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.120 align:middle line:90%
So T2 plus tau and so forth.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:28.500 align:middle line:84%
So we basically have that in
this window and in this window,

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.100 align:middle line:84%
the distribution of
outcomes is the same.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.820 align:middle line:90%
So what does that mean?

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:40.800 align:middle line:84%
Well, that means that if there's
a mean value in this period,

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.740 align:middle line:84%
then it's the same mean value
in this other period, as well

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.240 align:middle line:90%
as all possible periods.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:53.260 align:middle line:84%
So we basically have
a probability model

00:03:53.260 --> 00:04:01.010 align:middle line:84%
that is constrained in terms
of the level being horizontal

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:06.290 align:middle line:84%
and the amount of spread
about that level is constant.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:14.090 align:middle line:84%
Now with statistics, we
like to estimate parameters.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.470 align:middle line:84%
Parameters, like a sample mean,
are very easy to estimate.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:22.250 align:middle line:84%
We get a sample of values
and estimate the population

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:24.890 align:middle line:90%
mean with the sample mean.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:30.250 align:middle line:84%
And so we're maybe familiar
with estimating constants

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:33.390 align:middle line:84%
and estimating
means of populations

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.730 align:middle line:84%
is a trivial example or
a simple example of that.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:41.490 align:middle line:84%
With stationary
time series, we want

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:46.810 align:middle line:84%
to consider perhaps a
transform of our time series

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.490 align:middle line:90%
to a stationary scale.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:54.000 align:middle line:84%
And then there will be
parameters of that distribution

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.900 align:middle line:90%
that we can estimate.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:00.840 align:middle line:84%
And we can estimate them
because the distribution

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.100 align:middle line:84%
is the same, basically
across the whole range.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.600 align:middle line:84%
So there's some
hope that we'll be

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.800 align:middle line:84%
able to specify
parameters consistently

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.840 align:middle line:90%
and with high precision.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:24.240 align:middle line:84%
Now a less strict
stationarity condition

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.280 align:middle line:84%
is called covariance
stationarity.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.240 align:middle line:84%
And with covariance
stationarity,

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.440 align:middle line:84%
we're looking just at the first
and second order expectations

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.440 align:middle line:90%
of the time series values.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:47.480 align:middle line:84%
So the mean value of Xt will
be a constant mu for all t

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:55.340 align:middle line:84%
and the variance of Xt will
also not depend on the time t.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:59.520 align:middle line:84%
It will be stationary or
constant over the time range.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:07.920 align:middle line:84%
And the covariance of values
that are tau units apart

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.440 align:middle line:84%
will have a covariance that
simply is a function of how

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.720 align:middle line:90%
far apart the time points are.

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:24.520 align:middle line:84%
So here, we're looking basically
at a mean level mu and maybe

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:30.160 align:middle line:84%
plus or minus the square
root of the variance of Xt

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:36.420 align:middle line:84%
being the lower and
upper levels here.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.600 align:middle line:84%
And then if we're
interested in looking

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:43.960 align:middle line:84%
at how correlated
different time values are,

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:52.590 align:middle line:84%
then if we consider,
say, t and t plus tau,

00:06:52.590 --> 00:07:01.910 align:middle line:84%
I guess, and we also consider
another t prime and t prime plus

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:09.230 align:middle line:84%
tau over here, then when we have
realizations of the process,

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:13.430 align:middle line:84%
they're going to be staying
within a few standard deviations

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:14.830 align:middle line:90%
of the mean.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:21.310 align:middle line:84%
And the correlation between
values at different time points

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:27.990 align:middle line:84%
will be the same if the time
period between those time points

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:29.590 align:middle line:90%
are the same.

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:34.590 align:middle line:84%
So that's covariance
stationarity.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:41.510 align:middle line:84%
Now with such time series that
are covariance stationary, then

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:46.020 align:middle line:84%
we can look at the
autocorrelation function.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:52.340 align:middle line:84%
And the correlation
between Xt and Xt plus tau

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.500 align:middle line:84%
is the covariance
between the two

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:59.400 align:middle line:84%
divided by the square root of
the product of the variances.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:03.540 align:middle line:84%
So these are familiar formulas,
generally, for correlations.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.820 align:middle line:84%
And we call them
autocorrelations

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:09.180 align:middle line:84%
because we're looking at the
correlation of a time series

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.620 align:middle line:90%
with itself.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:18.500 align:middle line:84%
And so we have rho of t
is the autocorrelation.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:24.300 align:middle line:84%
Now let's take a look at
some financial time series.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:29.540 align:middle line:84%
Let's begin with just
looking at the S&P 500 index.

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:38.539 align:middle line:84%
So this is the value of the 500
major stocks in the US equity

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:41.220 align:middle line:90%
market.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:45.410 align:middle line:84%
This is from, I think,
2011 to 2020 plus.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.970 align:middle line:84%
And you can see how this,
over that period of time,

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:54.610 align:middle line:84%
trended up with some
dramatic drops due to COVID.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:59.770 align:middle line:84%
And with such a
time series, it's

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:05.790 align:middle line:84%
clearly not stationary because
it's trending over time.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.530 align:middle line:84%
If we looked at
different bands of years,

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:13.890 align:middle line:84%
they have different means, and
so we really cannot consider

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:18.570 align:middle line:84%
this scaling of the index to
be stationary or covariance

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:20.170 align:middle line:90%
stationary.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:26.050 align:middle line:84%
And what ends up happening with
many, many financial time series

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:33.450 align:middle line:84%
is that we consider the
time series Yt, say,

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:41.370 align:middle line:84%
to correspond to a random
walk model on the log scale.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:50.530 align:middle line:84%
So if we were to look at
taking the log of the series

00:09:50.530 --> 00:10:09.130 align:middle line:84%
and look at the
increments of that,

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:19.130 align:middle line:90%
this is the log return of Yt.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:26.690 align:middle line:84%
Then the value of Yt is
going to be an initial value

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:33.650 align:middle line:84%
Y0 times the exponential of
the sum of these log returns.

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:38.450 align:middle line:84%
And we can relate
these log returns

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:45.520 align:middle line:84%
to the percent returns
on a fractional scale.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:50.640 align:middle line:84%
This is log of 1
plus Rt, where Rt

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:59.490 align:middle line:84%
is equal to Yt minus Yt
minus 1 over Yt minus 1.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:04.120 align:middle line:90%


00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:08.080 align:middle line:84%
So what will
commonly be the case

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.120 align:middle line:84%
is that this sequence
of log returns

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:15.920 align:middle line:90%
is covariance stationary.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.040 align:middle line:84%
And so what I'd
like to do next is

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.280 align:middle line:84%
to look at some
time series plots

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:25.600 align:middle line:84%
of different
financial time series,

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:32.360 align:middle line:84%
and see whether this
transformation to log returns

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:36.280 align:middle line:84%
seems to make the series
appear stationary.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:42.070 align:middle line:90%
So here's the S&P 500 index.

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:43.950 align:middle line:84%
These graphs here
actually update

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:50.230 align:middle line:84%
the graph from before
to the end of September.

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:58.070 align:middle line:84%
And if we change initially
the absolute price

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:01.830 align:middle line:84%
to the log of the
price, you'll notice

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:09.510 align:middle line:84%
that an exponential growth tends
to transform to a linear growth

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:10.520 align:middle line:90%
on the log scale.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:14.630 align:middle line:90%


00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:18.110 align:middle line:84%
That comment is
representing the fact

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:21.710 align:middle line:84%
that the lower panel follows
more of a straight line

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:24.190 align:middle line:84%
path than the upper
panel, which has

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:27.870 align:middle line:84%
perhaps some exponential
curvature to it.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:34.510 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we look at the
monthly log returns,

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:44.220 align:middle line:84%
we can see that those monthly
increments on the log scale

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:48.620 align:middle line:84%
have basically a flat
time series process.

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:53.300 align:middle line:84%
And so it's rather
consistent with this movement

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:56.860 align:middle line:84%
across a level
that, in this case,

00:12:56.860 --> 00:13:02.900 align:middle line:84%
is close to zero with
constant variance.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:08.100 align:middle line:84%
Now, with this data,
we might consider

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:14.300 align:middle line:84%
looking at the distribution
of these log returns

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:18.520 align:middle line:84%
and see whether they're
consistent with a normal model,

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:22.420 align:middle line:84%
just as a benchmark
example that we might use.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:30.060 align:middle line:84%
And here's the fitted
normal curve to the data.

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:32.430 align:middle line:84%
Would you say that this
is a good fit to the data?

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:35.820 align:middle line:90%


00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:39.500 align:middle line:90%
Do I see any nods or shakes?

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:42.522 align:middle line:90%
I think you had a shake.

00:13:42.522 --> 00:13:43.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It looks
like relatively,

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:48.340 align:middle line:84%
but it follows like the up
and down, but [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:48.340 --> 00:13:49.340 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Right.

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:49.840 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:57.580 align:middle line:84%
And so the true height is
basically the realized values

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:01.580 align:middle line:84%
that are close to the
mean, and the normal fit

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:05.260 align:middle line:84%
is underestimating those
values that are close.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:10.180 align:middle line:84%
And complimenting that, the
tails of the distribution

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:14.180 align:middle line:84%
maybe are underestimating the
magnitude of the tails as well.

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:17.340 align:middle line:84%
And so this property
is a property

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:24.100 align:middle line:84%
that is called lepto
kurtosis, which means slender.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:28.740 align:middle line:90%
Lepto is Greek for slender.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:30.340 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a
look at what happens

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:36.890 align:middle line:84%
if we look at weekly returns
OK, with weekly returns,

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:41.490 align:middle line:84%
we get, again, a series that
appears to be fairly flat.

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:45.410 align:middle line:84%
The volatility, though, looks
like it maybe isn't stable.

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:52.290 align:middle line:84%
So the variation of the time
series at different time points

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:55.450 align:middle line:90%
may not be constant.

00:14:55.450 --> 00:15:00.050 align:middle line:84%
It certainly was extreme
during the COVID crisis,

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:01.490 align:middle line:90%
as evident here.

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:03.750 align:middle line:84%
And here's, again,
the normal curve,

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:07.290 align:middle line:84%
which has the same
properties noted before.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:11.170 align:middle line:84%
And then here's the
daily log returns.

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:14.410 align:middle line:84%
So looking at higher
frequency measurements,

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:20.410 align:middle line:84%
we, again, get this property
of stability in the mean

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:25.850 align:middle line:84%
but maybe not a stability or
stationarity in the variance.

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:29.450 align:middle line:84%
And again, the
normal model doesn't

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:32.440 align:middle line:90%
look like it's appropriate.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:34.980 align:middle line:84%
It captures a
center and a spread,

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:41.120 align:middle line:84%
but seems to be far off
near the mean and far away.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:45.120 align:middle line:84%
OK, let's take a
look at Amazon stock.

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:50.320 align:middle line:84%
So we're just going to look
at the same plots here.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:56.640 align:middle line:84%
You'll notice that the absolute
price has a rather notable

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:59.800 align:middle line:90%
exponential growth in it.

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:05.080 align:middle line:84%
When we take the log
scale, we have this plot.

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:08.320 align:middle line:84%
And what's really
important to see

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:16.800 align:middle line:84%
is whether the variability in
the time series is constant

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:18.280 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:24.080 align:middle line:84%
And before taking the logs, we
have a really narrow variation

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:28.520 align:middle line:84%
of the price series at the
beginning compared with the end.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:34.350 align:middle line:84%
But on the log scale, the
variation is more comparable.

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:38.910 align:middle line:84%
So that's actually a
good thing to have.

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:41.950 align:middle line:84%
We still have a
trend in the series,

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:50.110 align:middle line:84%
and taking the differences
in the log values

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:56.450 align:middle line:84%
makes this series stationary,
or at least appear stationary.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:17:02.750 align:middle line:84%
And here's the log returns
on a monthly frequency.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:08.750 align:middle line:84%
And here's the
histogram of those log

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:11.869 align:middle line:90%
returns and the normal fit.

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:18.050 align:middle line:84%
It's perhaps doing a slightly
better job than for the S&P 500.

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:20.750 align:middle line:90%
It's not clear.

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:23.990 align:middle line:84%
Here is the weekly
frequency time series.

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:28.470 align:middle line:84%
Again, the top panel
does look like it's

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:31.830 align:middle line:84%
consistent with a
stationary series in terms

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:35.030 align:middle line:84%
of the properties we've
highlighted so far.

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:38.270 align:middle line:84%
And the Gaussian
distribution seems

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:41.230 align:middle line:90%
to be fitting a bit better.

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:43.410 align:middle line:84%
If we look at the
daily log returns,

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:53.030 align:middle line:84%
then we have this lepto kurtosis
appearing in this histogram

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:54.590 align:middle line:90%
of daily returns.

00:17:54.590 --> 00:18:00.095 align:middle line:84%
And in looking at the time
series of the daily returns,

00:18:00.095 --> 00:18:04.990 align:middle line:84%
it looks like there are
some significant spikes that

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:09.450 align:middle line:84%
are not really consistent
with the rest of the series.

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:13.830 align:middle line:84%
So maybe that
feature is something

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:18.430 align:middle line:84%
that we would want to
incorporate into our models.

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:23.550 align:middle line:84%
Well here's, getting away
from the equity markets,

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:28.220 align:middle line:84%
this is looking at the
crude oil futures contract.

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:34.660 align:middle line:84%
And with the crude
oil futures contract,

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:40.900 align:middle line:84%
this future, it trades, I
think, at the Chicago Mercantile

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:43.380 align:middle line:90%
Exchange.

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:47.780 align:middle line:84%
It actually has a
dramatic feature

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:52.620 align:middle line:84%
that the value of the
contract went negative

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:58.180 align:middle line:84%
in 2020, which was a shock
to financial markets,

00:18:58.180 --> 00:19:03.740 align:middle line:84%
especially brokers and
traders in those contracts.

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:10.060 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine if you had a
hundred contracts of crude oil,

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:13.080 align:middle line:84%
on the day you woke up and
the price was negative,

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:18.380 align:middle line:84%
you basically owed your broker
100 times that negative value.

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:22.100 align:middle line:84%
And brokers actually
sometimes were

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:24.810 align:middle line:84%
unable to tell you
what your position was

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:29.010 align:middle line:84%
because their systems didn't
allow for negative prices.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:33.170 align:middle line:84%
If we tried to take
the log of the series

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:36.630 align:middle line:84%
and look at the differences,
with these negative values,

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:39.810 align:middle line:90%
that's actually a huge problem.

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:42.690 align:middle line:90%
But nonetheless, let's see.

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:47.490 align:middle line:84%
I did graph these,
which essentially

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:51.690 align:middle line:84%
will exclude those days when
there were negative values

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:53.250 align:middle line:90%
for the contract.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:58.690 align:middle line:84%
And looking at this, here's
the monthly log returns.

00:19:58.690 --> 00:20:05.690 align:middle line:84%
So this is excluding the
negative price periods

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:11.750 align:middle line:84%
on a monthly basis, on a weekly
basis, and on a daily basis.

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:15.050 align:middle line:84%
And so what's really
important here

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:19.970 align:middle line:84%
is just how looking at
different financial time

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:27.040 align:middle line:84%
series with the same
framework of logging

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:31.520 align:middle line:84%
the series if it's positive and
looking at increments on the log

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:36.640 align:middle line:84%
scale, which correspond
to percentage changes,

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:41.120 align:middle line:84%
the data generally is
transformed or often

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:44.070 align:middle line:84%
is transformed to
a stationary scale.

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:46.800 align:middle line:90%


00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:55.960 align:middle line:84%
Yet another example is looking
at the yields of the 10 year US

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:58.440 align:middle line:90%
Treasury.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:02.680 align:middle line:90%
And so the top panel shows--

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:05.840 align:middle line:84%
it's called price,
but it's really

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:12.840 align:middle line:84%
the yield in percentage terms
of 10 year government bonds.

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:16.640 align:middle line:84%
And so you can see
it started above 3%,

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:21.800 align:middle line:84%
dropped down to less than 1%
following the COVID crisis,

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:25.240 align:middle line:90%
and then was rising ever since.

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:31.760 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at the
price or the yield changes

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:34.460 align:middle line:84%
on the log scale,
we get this plot,

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:39.760 align:middle line:84%
which isn't too different
in its appearance.

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:50.600 align:middle line:84%
But then if we look at the
changes in log, the increments

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:53.520 align:middle line:90%
in log, which are log returns--

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:56.640 align:middle line:84%
in mathematical
definition, log returns

00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:01.400 align:middle line:84%
are not really the right
terminology for the percentage

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:02.880 align:middle line:90%
changes in yield.

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:07.160 align:middle line:84%
But we'll basically be using
the same terminology as before.

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:10.200 align:middle line:84%
One can see that
we basically get

00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:14.040 align:middle line:84%
a distribution of
increments, steps

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:16.200 align:middle line:84%
in the random walk
on the log scale

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:21.670 align:middle line:90%
that are unimodal and symmetric.

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:26.010 align:middle line:84%
And as we consider
higher frequency--

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.170 align:middle line:90%
here's weekly and here's daily--

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:31.550 align:middle line:84%
it's actually doing a
better job, perhaps,

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:35.710 align:middle line:84%
using a normal model for
this transformed series

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:38.830 align:middle line:84%
than for the equity
market series.

00:22:38.830 --> 00:22:47.630 align:middle line:84%
So with these cases, the same
manipulations of the data,

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:50.430 align:middle line:84%
taking logs, looking
at log returns,

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:58.030 align:middle line:84%
corresponds to transforming the
series to a random walk process.

00:22:58.030 --> 00:23:06.270 align:middle line:84%
And with random walk processes,
we can think of the sum from 0

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:08.710 align:middle line:90%
to t of--

00:23:08.710 --> 00:23:18.140 align:middle line:84%
or the capital TC of X sub t
and call this maybe S sub t.

00:23:18.140 --> 00:23:24.780 align:middle line:84%
In modeling the random
walk by this sum,

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:30.860 align:middle line:84%
there will be many different
models we can consider for this.

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:36.540 align:middle line:84%
The simplest model would be
one where these X's, the steps,

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:41.940 align:middle line:84%
are identically distributed
and independent.

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:46.580 align:middle line:90%
So it's like a pure random walk.

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:56.260 align:middle line:84%
And more complicated models will
consider St or the Xt's to be

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:58.820 align:middle line:90%
dependent upon each other.

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:03.100 align:middle line:84%
So what we'd like
to do, then, is--

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:03.620 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:08.380 align:middle line:90%


00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:10.780 align:middle line:90%
Let's see here.

00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:15.180 align:middle line:84%
If we think of this
autocorrelation function,

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:25.690 align:middle line:84%
the correlation between X's that
are tau units of time apart,

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:30.730 align:middle line:84%
we can actually calculate
autocorrelations

00:24:30.730 --> 00:24:34.630 align:middle line:84%
for each of the series
we just looked at.

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:39.170 align:middle line:84%
And so here is the
autocorrelation function

00:24:39.170 --> 00:24:44.610 align:middle line:84%
of the monthly log
returns on the S&P 500.

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:49.130 align:middle line:84%
And one can see
that there actually

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:54.010 align:middle line:84%
was a large negative
autocorrelation

00:24:54.010 --> 00:24:56.890 align:middle line:90%
for one month apart.

00:24:56.890 --> 00:25:03.570 align:middle line:84%
And the other autocorrelations
varied around this blue range.

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:09.890 align:middle line:84%
And what's important
with autocorrelations,

00:25:09.890 --> 00:25:20.290 align:middle line:84%
if we have a time series Xt, t
equaling 1, 2, up to capital T,

00:25:20.290 --> 00:25:35.650 align:middle line:84%
and then we consider the sample
autocorrelation at lag K,

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:40.530 align:middle line:84%
then this is an estimate of
the correlation between Xt

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:44.490 align:middle line:90%
and Xt plus K.

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:47.290 align:middle line:90%
And we call this RK.

00:25:47.290 --> 00:25:49.850 align:middle line:84%
And I'll maybe put a hat
on it to indicate that this

00:25:49.850 --> 00:25:52.810 align:middle line:90%
is estimated from the sample.

00:25:52.810 --> 00:26:03.290 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that this is
approximately distributed

00:26:03.290 --> 00:26:09.010 align:middle line:90%
as a normal with a mean--

00:26:09.010 --> 00:26:14.400 align:middle line:84%
or I guess, OK, a
normal with the rho K,

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:20.640 align:middle line:84%
the true autocorrelation,
and a variance that's

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:32.920 align:middle line:84%
1 over T minus K. So if
we look at these bands,

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:37.520 align:middle line:84%
these bands correspond
to the region

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:42.250 align:middle line:84%
of sample autocorrelations that
are consistent with a zero.

00:26:42.250 --> 00:26:45.080 align:middle line:90%


00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:49.216 align:middle line:90%
True autocorrelation.

00:26:49.216 --> 00:26:55.400 align:middle line:84%
If we want to test a null
hypothesis that rho K is

00:26:55.400 --> 00:27:08.800 align:middle line:84%
equal to zero, we can say reject
if R hat K is greater than 1.96

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:11.910 align:middle line:84%
times the square root
of 1 over T minus K,

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:17.850 align:middle line:84%
applying a normal model for
that return distribution.

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:21.990 align:middle line:84%
So we basically
have, for the R hat

00:27:21.990 --> 00:27:27.190 align:middle line:84%
K where we have a rho K
here, we have a bell shaped

00:27:27.190 --> 00:27:33.670 align:middle line:84%
curve with a standard deviation
square root of 1 over T minus K.

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:38.230 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider
rho K equal to zero,

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:42.110 align:middle line:84%
then we do the rejection if
it's outside these bands.

00:27:42.110 --> 00:27:47.390 align:middle line:90%


00:27:47.390 --> 00:27:50.510 align:middle line:84%
Let's take a look at how
these autocorrelations

00:27:50.510 --> 00:27:53.710 align:middle line:90%
vary as we vary the frequency.

00:27:53.710 --> 00:28:02.190 align:middle line:84%
Here's the weekly frequency for
the computation of log returns.

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:07.310 align:middle line:84%
And we have this pattern
where perhaps there

00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:15.100 align:middle line:84%
are some significant negative
autocorrelations at, say,

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:18.260 align:middle line:90%
five weeks apart.

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:27.060 align:middle line:84%
And what would you say is
a property of a time series

00:28:27.060 --> 00:28:31.020 align:middle line:84%
that has negative
autocorrelations?

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:34.860 align:middle line:90%
Say, of order at lag 5?

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:39.040 align:middle line:84%
What does that really mean in
terms of what you would expect?

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:39.873 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:28:39.873 --> 00:28:41.040 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: So mean reversion.

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:41.840 align:middle line:90%
Mean reverting.

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:42.757 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yes.

00:28:42.757 --> 00:28:43.740 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:49.300 align:middle line:84%
So the negative autocorrelations
are consistent, perhaps,

00:28:49.300 --> 00:28:50.920 align:middle line:90%
with mean reversion.

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:58.620 align:middle line:84%
So that means that if the
price changes are too high,

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:00.640 align:middle line:84%
they'll revert back,
or if they're too low,

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:01.550 align:middle line:90%
they'll revert back.

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:04.620 align:middle line:90%


00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:05.260 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:10.640 align:middle line:84%
Also on these graphs are
partial autocorrelations,

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:14.780 align:middle line:90%
which we'll discuss later.

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:21.140 align:middle line:84%
But partial autocorrelations
are correlations at a given

00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:28.900 align:middle line:84%
lag after accommodating the
dependence on lower order lags.

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:33.540 align:middle line:84%
We'll see how that works
out in the case study later.

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:37.500 align:middle line:84%
But let's take a look at
just these other series.

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:42.780 align:middle line:84%
So here's the daily
series with the S&P 500.

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:45.020 align:middle line:84%
And so you can see,
oh, there's actually

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:50.420 align:middle line:84%
some reasonable strong
autocorrelations.

00:29:50.420 --> 00:29:53.140 align:middle line:90%
Here's Amazon stock.

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:56.470 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure why it doesn't
have the band on the lower side.

00:29:56.470 --> 00:29:59.020 align:middle line:90%


00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:03.780 align:middle line:84%
But it basically is
symmetrical with that blue band

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:09.250 align:middle line:84%
where the correlation
is consistent with zero

00:30:09.250 --> 00:30:11.290 align:middle line:90%
autocorrelation.

00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:13.170 align:middle line:90%
Here's the weekly.

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:15.010 align:middle line:90%
Here's the daily.

00:30:15.010 --> 00:30:17.770 align:middle line:84%
So interestingly,
Amazon stock doesn't

00:30:17.770 --> 00:30:21.730 align:middle line:84%
seem to have any significant
autocorrelations, regardless

00:30:21.730 --> 00:30:23.250 align:middle line:90%
of the frequency.

00:30:23.250 --> 00:30:27.810 align:middle line:90%
Here's the crude oil future.

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:32.710 align:middle line:84%
You can see that with the crude
oil future, on a weekly basis,

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:37.290 align:middle line:84%
we have a negative
autocorrelation at two weeks

00:30:37.290 --> 00:30:40.330 align:middle line:84%
and then a positive
autocorrelation at three weeks.

00:30:40.330 --> 00:30:44.170 align:middle line:84%
So there's some
interesting patterns

00:30:44.170 --> 00:30:47.850 align:middle line:90%
that perhaps can be exploited.

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:49.770 align:middle line:90%
And here's daily.

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:54.610 align:middle line:84%
Again, one can see the rather
significant autocorrelations

00:30:54.610 --> 00:30:58.850 align:middle line:90%
at the low lag values.

00:30:58.850 --> 00:31:04.680 align:middle line:84%
And let's see with
the Treasury yields.

00:31:04.680 --> 00:31:10.160 align:middle line:84%
Looking at the autocorrelations
and how the log yield changes,

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:12.920 align:middle line:84%
one can see that there
is some strong time

00:31:12.920 --> 00:31:19.360 align:middle line:84%
dependence at the weekly basis,
as well as the daily basis.

00:31:19.360 --> 00:31:22.060 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:25.400 align:middle line:84%
This discussion of
autocorrelations

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:33.720 align:middle line:84%
is a very important one
in time series modeling.

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:43.800 align:middle line:84%
And I just want to highlight
how, if we have a time series

00:31:43.800 --> 00:31:48.170 align:middle line:90%
and we fit a time series model.

00:31:48.170 --> 00:31:52.400 align:middle line:90%


00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:59.140 align:middle line:84%
And so we compute, say,
Y hat t, t equals 1 to T,

00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:09.990 align:middle line:84%
then the residual series from
this fit of the time series

00:32:09.990 --> 00:32:18.130 align:middle line:84%
model is the set of errors
which are Yt minus Y hat t.

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:23.470 align:middle line:90%


00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:29.070 align:middle line:84%
And we can just write
Yt is equal to Y

00:32:29.070 --> 00:32:35.230 align:middle line:90%
hat t plus epsilon or error t.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:36.260 align:middle line:90%
A good model.

00:32:36.260 --> 00:32:38.790 align:middle line:90%


00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:52.030 align:middle line:84%
So the model is good
if the et's basically

00:32:52.030 --> 00:33:03.830 align:middle line:84%
have mean zero,
constant variance,

00:33:03.830 --> 00:33:11.550 align:middle line:84%
are uncorrelated
with each other.

00:33:11.550 --> 00:33:17.110 align:middle line:84%
And so these assumptions
are basically

00:33:17.110 --> 00:33:19.570 align:middle line:84%
assumptions of a
white noise process.

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:24.750 align:middle line:90%


00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:31.870 align:middle line:84%
So if our model
basically predicts up

00:33:31.870 --> 00:33:40.110 align:middle line:84%
to this residual process,
which is unpredictable,

00:33:40.110 --> 00:33:43.050 align:middle line:84%
has mean zero, constant
variance, is uncorrelated,

00:33:43.050 --> 00:33:46.630 align:middle line:84%
there's no information left
in the residual series.

00:33:46.630 --> 00:33:51.510 align:middle line:84%
And so we can use
the autocorrelation

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:55.830 align:middle line:90%
of the residual series.

00:33:55.830 --> 00:34:03.700 align:middle line:84%
We can use the autocorrelation
function of the error series

00:34:03.700 --> 00:34:13.139 align:middle line:84%
to test if the model
is good, basically.

00:34:13.139 --> 00:34:17.739 align:middle line:84%
And a good model
will be a model that

00:34:17.739 --> 00:34:22.820 align:middle line:84%
has captured all of the time
dependent information in it,

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:31.460 align:middle line:84%
and the predictions from these
or forecasts from this model

00:34:31.460 --> 00:34:34.500 align:middle line:90%
are ones we have confidence in.

00:34:34.500 --> 00:34:38.650 align:middle line:84%
And so there's actually another
assumption here, which is that--

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:42.460 align:middle line:90%


00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:43.932 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:34:43.932 --> 00:34:52.460 align:middle line:84%
If the epsilon t are
normal or Gaussian,

00:34:52.460 --> 00:34:55.360 align:middle line:84%
then this gives
good predictions.

00:34:55.360 --> 00:34:58.250 align:middle line:90%


00:34:58.250 --> 00:35:03.530 align:middle line:84%
And by predictions, I'm
thinking of not point forecasts

00:35:03.530 --> 00:35:07.370 align:middle line:84%
of a series, but a prediction
interval and how wide should

00:35:07.370 --> 00:35:10.550 align:middle line:84%
that prediction interval be
depending upon the variability.

00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:14.330 align:middle line:90%


00:35:14.330 --> 00:35:16.210 align:middle line:84%
When we work with
time series, we're

00:35:16.210 --> 00:35:22.570 align:middle line:84%
actually are going to work
often with autocorrelations

00:35:22.570 --> 00:35:28.170 align:middle line:84%
of residuals to see
whether there's any time

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:29.520 align:middle line:90%
dependence in the series.

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:35.530 align:middle line:90%


00:35:35.530 --> 00:35:37.810 align:middle line:84%
What's rather
interesting to note

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:45.890 align:middle line:84%
is that one can fix
a single correlation,

00:35:45.890 --> 00:35:51.330 align:middle line:84%
lag, and use that to
test whether the data are

00:35:51.330 --> 00:35:55.600 align:middle line:84%
consistent with a zero
autocorrelation of a fixed lag.

00:35:55.600 --> 00:36:02.040 align:middle line:84%
We can also use sets
of lags and test

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:05.950 align:middle line:84%
whether the whole set
is equal to zero or not.

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:10.360 align:middle line:90%


00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:11.920 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:14.560 align:middle line:90%
Let me just write it over here.

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:27.760 align:middle line:84%
So if, say, rho 1 equals rho 2
equals rho k is equal to zero,

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:35.000 align:middle line:84%
then R hat J, which is
distributed as normal zero,

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:49.960 align:middle line:84%
roughly 1 over t for J equaling
1 to K. R hat J squared times t

00:36:49.960 --> 00:36:55.040 align:middle line:84%
is approximately the
square of a normal 0,

00:36:55.040 --> 00:36:59.960 align:middle line:84%
1, which is a chi squared
1 random variable.

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:09.440 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at the sum of
TR hat J squared from 1 to K,

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:12.960 align:middle line:84%
this will be approximately
a chi squared distribution

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:14.270 align:middle line:90%
with K degrees of freedom.

00:37:14.270 --> 00:37:18.280 align:middle line:90%


00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:30.000 align:middle line:84%
We can reject the null
hypothesis if this test

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:32.880 align:middle line:90%
statistic, I'll call it--

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:36.200 align:middle line:84%
well, actually, I'll call
it BP because it's actually

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:38.360 align:middle line:90%
called the Box-Pierce test.

00:37:38.360 --> 00:37:40.990 align:middle line:90%
If BP is large.

00:37:40.990 --> 00:37:43.595 align:middle line:90%


00:37:43.595 --> 00:37:48.840 align:middle line:84%
We'll say larger than
a critical value C

00:37:48.840 --> 00:37:55.170 align:middle line:84%
star, where this is determined
by the chi squared distribution.

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:58.910 align:middle line:90%


00:37:58.910 --> 00:38:02.260 align:middle line:84%
And so this is actually
called the Box-Pierce test.

00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:07.430 align:middle line:90%


00:38:07.430 --> 00:38:15.790 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll actually be very
interested in autocorrelations

00:38:15.790 --> 00:38:18.310 align:middle line:84%
of the original series
but then autocorrelations

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:20.390 align:middle line:90%
of the residual series.

00:38:20.390 --> 00:38:24.390 align:middle line:84%
And whenever we have a model
where the residual series still

00:38:24.390 --> 00:38:27.290 align:middle line:84%
has significant
autocorrelations in it,

00:38:27.290 --> 00:38:29.250 align:middle line:90%
then we're really not done yet.

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:30.640 align:middle line:90%
We really need to continue.

00:38:30.640 --> 00:38:36.510 align:middle line:90%


00:38:36.510 --> 00:38:38.710 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:38:38.710 --> 00:38:46.590 align:middle line:84%
So we have-- now, the next
topic is a really extraordinary

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:48.430 align:middle line:90%
theorem.

00:38:48.430 --> 00:38:51.660 align:middle line:90%
The Wold representation theorem.

00:38:51.660 --> 00:39:02.180 align:middle line:84%
And with stationary processes
that are covariance stationary,

00:39:02.180 --> 00:39:07.660 align:middle line:84%
then it turns out that any
covariance stationary process

00:39:07.660 --> 00:39:12.860 align:middle line:84%
can be decomposed into
a Vt process which

00:39:12.860 --> 00:39:16.500 align:middle line:90%
is linearly deterministic.

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:22.180 align:middle line:84%
So that means that
basically the current value

00:39:22.180 --> 00:39:27.620 align:middle line:84%
Vt as a linear combination
of past values.

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:33.540 align:middle line:84%
And St is a moving
average process

00:39:33.540 --> 00:39:40.270 align:middle line:84%
where we have these eta
t's which are white noise.

00:39:40.270 --> 00:39:43.100 align:middle line:90%


00:39:43.100 --> 00:39:48.530 align:middle line:84%
And our St process is
just a weighted sum

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:50.700 align:middle line:90%
of those white noise terms.

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:53.250 align:middle line:90%


00:39:53.250 --> 00:39:57.210 align:middle line:84%
And the white noise
terms are also

00:39:57.210 --> 00:40:03.690 align:middle line:84%
uncorrelated with the linearly
deterministic process.

00:40:03.690 --> 00:40:07.650 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:40:07.650 --> 00:40:10.730 align:middle line:84%
When I first learned
of this theorem,

00:40:10.730 --> 00:40:17.890 align:middle line:84%
I had an issue with, well, what
is a linearly deterministic

00:40:17.890 --> 00:40:19.450 align:middle line:90%
process?

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:23.680 align:middle line:84%
So can anyone suggest what a
linearly deterministic process

00:40:23.680 --> 00:40:24.180 align:middle line:90%
is?

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:27.970 align:middle line:90%


00:40:27.970 --> 00:40:30.760 align:middle line:84%
Or could be, or
an example of one?

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:43.890 align:middle line:90%


00:40:43.890 --> 00:40:51.110 align:middle line:84%
So we have Vt is
linearly deterministic.

00:40:51.110 --> 00:41:01.330 align:middle line:90%


00:41:01.330 --> 00:41:03.330 align:middle line:90%
So if we have Vt--

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:13.650 align:middle line:84%
so if we observe Vt over a
sequence of time points, well,

00:41:13.650 --> 00:41:20.970 align:middle line:84%
for what patterns of Vt that
determine the Vt process

00:41:20.970 --> 00:41:21.720 align:middle line:90%
into the future?

00:41:21.720 --> 00:41:24.730 align:middle line:90%


00:41:24.730 --> 00:41:25.680 align:middle line:90%
Can you think of any?

00:41:25.680 --> 00:41:32.650 align:middle line:90%


00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:45.080 align:middle line:84%
So if Vt is equal to mu
plus, say, cosine of theta t,

00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:48.520 align:middle line:84%
then this thing will
just move up and down,

00:41:48.520 --> 00:41:53.480 align:middle line:84%
and we can actually determine
that cosine function

00:41:53.480 --> 00:41:56.200 align:middle line:90%
from enough values of Vt.

00:41:56.200 --> 00:41:57.280 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:41:57.280 --> 00:42:00.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So does that mean
like any periodic process

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:02.760 align:middle line:90%
would be linearly determined?

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:03.880 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yes.

00:42:03.880 --> 00:42:05.120 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:42:05.120 --> 00:42:11.560 align:middle line:84%
And it's interesting
to know that.

00:42:11.560 --> 00:42:18.880 align:middle line:84%
I mean, those of you who may
have studied harmonic analysis

00:42:18.880 --> 00:42:24.520 align:middle line:84%
could come across
the result that if we

00:42:24.520 --> 00:42:32.240 align:middle line:84%
have, say, different
cosine or sine functions

00:42:32.240 --> 00:42:36.400 align:middle line:84%
and take sums of those,
then that will also

00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:40.120 align:middle line:84%
be a linearly
predictable process.

00:42:40.120 --> 00:42:47.050 align:middle line:84%
And so if you look at summing
signs of different frequencies,

00:42:47.050 --> 00:42:52.670 align:middle line:84%
which basically corresponds to
Fourier series that are flat,

00:42:52.670 --> 00:42:58.630 align:middle line:84%
then those are
linearly predictable.

00:42:58.630 --> 00:43:06.150 align:middle line:84%
So that's the Vt process
that we should be aware of.

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:09.070 align:middle line:84%
But what's really interesting
is that the variation

00:43:09.070 --> 00:43:14.830 align:middle line:84%
about that deterministic
process, the St process,

00:43:14.830 --> 00:43:21.070 align:middle line:84%
can be expressed as a
moving average process.

00:43:21.070 --> 00:43:28.350 align:middle line:84%
And this representation holds
for any covariance stationary

00:43:28.350 --> 00:43:29.710 align:middle line:90%
time series.

00:43:29.710 --> 00:43:32.300 align:middle line:84%
Now for any covariance
stationary time series--

00:43:32.300 --> 00:43:35.670 align:middle line:90%


00:43:35.670 --> 00:43:38.560 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:49.180 align:middle line:84%
With the covariance
stationary process St,

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:52.460 align:middle line:84%
we have basically that mu is
equal to the expected value

00:43:52.460 --> 00:43:59.300 align:middle line:84%
of St. Sigma squared, which
is the variance of St. This

00:43:59.300 --> 00:44:01.420 align:middle line:90%
is constant over time.

00:44:01.420 --> 00:44:03.700 align:middle line:90%
This is constant over time.

00:44:03.700 --> 00:44:09.100 align:middle line:84%
And the covariance
of Xt, Xt plus tau,

00:44:09.100 --> 00:44:18.180 align:middle line:84%
is equal to gamma
of tau for all t.

00:44:18.180 --> 00:44:25.220 align:middle line:84%
So any covariance
stationary process

00:44:25.220 --> 00:44:31.060 align:middle line:84%
can be represented as this
moving average of white noise

00:44:31.060 --> 00:44:31.560 align:middle line:90%
process.

00:44:31.560 --> 00:44:35.260 align:middle line:90%


00:44:35.260 --> 00:44:41.340 align:middle line:84%
And so, well, how would we
use this Wold representation

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:42.580 align:middle line:90%
theorem?

00:44:42.580 --> 00:44:45.940 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we have
a time series Xt

00:44:45.940 --> 00:44:50.780 align:middle line:84%
that we want to specify a
covariance stationary time

00:44:50.780 --> 00:44:55.100 align:middle line:84%
series model to it, well, the
first thing we would do perhaps

00:44:55.100 --> 00:45:00.500 align:middle line:84%
is de-trend the
series Xt so that it

00:45:00.500 --> 00:45:04.180 align:middle line:90%
has a constant mean level.

00:45:04.180 --> 00:45:10.140 align:middle line:84%
So if we had a linear
trend or a quadratic trend,

00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:12.360 align:middle line:90%
we would subtract that out.

00:45:12.360 --> 00:45:19.580 align:middle line:84%
So Xt is a series that looks
like it has a flat path.

00:45:19.580 --> 00:45:24.540 align:middle line:84%
And then we could initialize
a parameter p indicating

00:45:24.540 --> 00:45:31.500 align:middle line:84%
the past number of observations
that explain the linearly

00:45:31.500 --> 00:45:34.540 align:middle line:90%
deterministic term.

00:45:34.540 --> 00:45:41.410 align:middle line:84%
And we can then project
our time series Xt

00:45:41.410 --> 00:45:45.490 align:middle line:84%
onto the space
spanned by the lags,

00:45:45.490 --> 00:45:55.490 align:middle line:84%
the p lags, and then look at the
errors about that projection.

00:45:55.490 --> 00:46:04.290 align:middle line:84%
So here we can think of having
the time series values Y1 to Yn,

00:46:04.290 --> 00:46:10.850 align:middle line:84%
and we can project those onto
the lags of the Y values.

00:46:10.850 --> 00:46:14.730 align:middle line:84%
So for the first p
rows, we actually

00:46:14.730 --> 00:46:16.690 align:middle line:90%
need to go back in time.

00:46:16.690 --> 00:46:19.530 align:middle line:84%
So that means we
have to start not

00:46:19.530 --> 00:46:23.610 align:middle line:84%
from the beginning of our time
series, but maybe p units ahead.

00:46:23.610 --> 00:46:26.370 align:middle line:84%
And then we can apply
ordinary least squares

00:46:26.370 --> 00:46:34.010 align:middle line:84%
to define the projection matrix
onto those lagged values use.

00:46:34.010 --> 00:46:38.720 align:middle line:84%
And then we have this
projected residual.

00:46:38.720 --> 00:46:45.080 align:middle line:84%
And what we would want is
that this projected residual,

00:46:45.080 --> 00:46:49.160 align:middle line:84%
if it's a stationary
time series,

00:46:49.160 --> 00:46:52.080 align:middle line:84%
we could use time
series methods we'll

00:46:52.080 --> 00:46:55.080 align:middle line:84%
learn about how to
specify a moving

00:46:55.080 --> 00:47:01.640 align:middle line:84%
average model to these series
and get estimates of the size

00:47:01.640 --> 00:47:06.640 align:middle line:84%
and also estimates of
the white noise terms.

00:47:06.640 --> 00:47:14.680 align:middle line:84%
And with this step,
we then can evaluate

00:47:14.680 --> 00:47:22.840 align:middle line:84%
whether our residuals are
orthogonal to lagged values

00:47:22.840 --> 00:47:26.120 align:middle line:90%
for lags greater than p back.

00:47:26.120 --> 00:47:36.310 align:middle line:84%
So these residuals should not
have any dependence on lags of y

00:47:36.310 --> 00:47:38.790 align:middle line:90%
that are further back than p.

00:47:38.790 --> 00:47:42.550 align:middle line:90%


00:47:42.550 --> 00:47:46.910 align:middle line:84%
If those are not orthogonal,
then we should increase p.

00:47:46.910 --> 00:47:50.550 align:middle line:84%
And then we want to
look at whether the eta

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:53.310 align:middle line:84%
t's in our moving average
model, whether those

00:47:53.310 --> 00:47:57.350 align:middle line:84%
are consistent with
white noise or not.

00:47:57.350 --> 00:48:00.230 align:middle line:90%
And so if they are, we're good.

00:48:00.230 --> 00:48:04.270 align:middle line:84%
But if not, then
we need to consider

00:48:04.270 --> 00:48:08.430 align:middle line:84%
changing the specification
of the moving average model

00:48:08.430 --> 00:48:11.470 align:middle line:84%
and perhaps adding
other variables

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:13.590 align:middle line:84%
such as deterministic
variables that could

00:48:13.590 --> 00:48:16.230 align:middle line:90%
be useful in the projections.

00:48:16.230 --> 00:48:19.030 align:middle line:84%
But what this
demonstrates is how

00:48:19.030 --> 00:48:24.730 align:middle line:84%
this theory can,
in the abstract,

00:48:24.730 --> 00:48:27.640 align:middle line:90%
be applied to real world data.

00:48:27.640 --> 00:48:31.990 align:middle line:90%


00:48:31.990 --> 00:48:39.750 align:middle line:84%
So as we increase p, the
number of lags we use

00:48:39.750 --> 00:48:47.950 align:middle line:84%
for the projection, we end up
getting a limiting value as p

00:48:47.950 --> 00:48:55.630 align:middle line:84%
gets large, that this is
capturing the linearly

00:48:55.630 --> 00:49:00.194 align:middle line:84%
predictable part of
the series, the Vt's.

00:49:00.194 --> 00:49:05.030 align:middle line:84%
But this assumption
basically requires

00:49:05.030 --> 00:49:08.670 align:middle line:84%
if we let p grow
arbitrarily large,

00:49:08.670 --> 00:49:17.330 align:middle line:84%
we probably want to make sure
that p over n goes to zero,

00:49:17.330 --> 00:49:21.310 align:middle line:84%
so that we're
estimating p parameters

00:49:21.310 --> 00:49:28.380 align:middle line:84%
with a number of points
that is large relative to p.

00:49:28.380 --> 00:49:32.140 align:middle line:90%


00:49:32.140 --> 00:49:34.660 align:middle line:84%
So this is just
a way of thinking

00:49:34.660 --> 00:49:40.560 align:middle line:84%
about how we might approach
our modeling effort,

00:49:40.560 --> 00:49:44.020 align:middle line:84%
exploiting the
Wold decomposition.

00:49:44.020 --> 00:49:47.500 align:middle line:84%
But what's really
important here is

00:49:47.500 --> 00:49:53.340 align:middle line:84%
that any covariant
stationary model

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:56.100 align:middle line:84%
has a representation
as this moving

00:49:56.100 --> 00:49:59.820 align:middle line:90%
average process of white noise.

00:49:59.820 --> 00:50:03.420 align:middle line:84%
So that's a fairly
powerful result.

00:50:03.420 --> 00:50:07.020 align:middle line:84%
It says that once we
have transformed our data

00:50:07.020 --> 00:50:11.140 align:middle line:84%
to a scale where covariant
stationarity is appropriate,

00:50:11.140 --> 00:50:17.620 align:middle line:84%
then we can restrict ourselves
to the class of moving

00:50:17.620 --> 00:50:19.540 align:middle line:90%
average processes.

00:50:19.540 --> 00:50:23.420 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll see is that
there are alternatives

00:50:23.420 --> 00:50:27.130 align:middle line:84%
to moving average processes
called autoregressions

00:50:27.130 --> 00:50:33.890 align:middle line:84%
that are very closely related
to moving average processes.

00:50:33.890 --> 00:50:35.850 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:50:35.850 --> 00:50:39.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I guess my
question is, why do you

00:50:39.210 --> 00:50:43.130 align:middle line:84%
need to specify
covariance stationarity

00:50:43.130 --> 00:50:47.850 align:middle line:90%
for the representation theorem?

00:50:47.850 --> 00:50:50.832 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: OK.

00:50:50.832 --> 00:50:52.590 align:middle line:90%
Well, with the representation--

00:50:52.590 --> 00:50:53.090 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:50:53.090 --> 00:50:56.810 align:middle line:90%


00:50:56.810 --> 00:50:58.910 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:50:58.910 --> 00:50:59.410 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:50:59.410 --> 00:51:00.310 align:middle line:90%
Where is it here?

00:51:00.310 --> 00:51:01.150 align:middle line:90%
No, it's fine.

00:51:01.150 --> 00:51:01.930 align:middle line:90%
Here it is.

00:51:01.930 --> 00:51:07.290 align:middle line:84%
Basically, if we
consider this process

00:51:07.290 --> 00:51:12.450 align:middle line:84%
St, which has some set
of weights psi i times

00:51:12.450 --> 00:51:17.290 align:middle line:84%
white noise eta t's,
then if these conditions

00:51:17.290 --> 00:51:20.970 align:middle line:90%
are satisfied for the psis--

00:51:20.970 --> 00:51:26.840 align:middle line:84%
so the sum, they're
square summable, finite.

00:51:26.840 --> 00:51:35.120 align:middle line:84%
Then this process St is going
to be covariant stationary.

00:51:35.120 --> 00:51:38.780 align:middle line:84%
It'll have finite mean and
variance that's constant,

00:51:38.780 --> 00:51:43.240 align:middle line:84%
and the covariances will also
be constant over increments

00:51:43.240 --> 00:51:45.840 align:middle line:90%
of any lag.

00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:51.800 align:middle line:84%
What's powerful is that
this representation

00:51:51.800 --> 00:51:57.040 align:middle line:84%
can be used for any
covariant stationary model.

00:51:57.040 --> 00:51:59.960 align:middle line:90%
So this is covariant stationary.

00:51:59.960 --> 00:52:02.760 align:middle line:84%
But any covariant
stationary model

00:52:02.760 --> 00:52:06.400 align:middle line:90%
has this kind of representation.

00:52:06.400 --> 00:52:07.200 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:52:07.200 --> 00:52:09.480 align:middle line:90%
Sure.

00:52:09.480 --> 00:52:15.120 align:middle line:84%
And of course, you know,
covariant stationarity

00:52:15.120 --> 00:52:16.180 align:middle line:90%
is a nice assumption.

00:52:16.180 --> 00:52:18.280 align:middle line:90%
It may not be satisfied.

00:52:18.280 --> 00:52:21.180 align:middle line:84%
So you need to have
more complex models.

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:25.720 align:middle line:84%
But it's good to know when you
have these assumptions satisfied

00:52:25.720 --> 00:52:28.400 align:middle line:90%
that this is the representation.

00:52:28.400 --> 00:52:28.900 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:52:28.900 --> 00:52:33.640 align:middle line:84%
So we next want to introduce you
to this operator called a lag

00:52:33.640 --> 00:52:37.560 align:middle line:90%
operator or backshift operator.

00:52:37.560 --> 00:52:43.280 align:middle line:84%
And because I call it a
lag operator, I label it L.

00:52:43.280 --> 00:52:48.680 align:middle line:84%
And so L operates
on a time series Xt

00:52:48.680 --> 00:52:53.720 align:middle line:84%
and just shifts the time
index back one period.

00:52:53.720 --> 00:52:57.440 align:middle line:84%
So we're thinking of
discrete time series

00:52:57.440 --> 00:53:06.360 align:middle line:84%
with integer values of t
and the lag operating on Xt

00:53:06.360 --> 00:53:10.800 align:middle line:84%
shifts the time units
back one period.

00:53:10.800 --> 00:53:19.544 align:middle line:84%
And we can think of L to the
0 power doing the identity.

00:53:19.544 --> 00:53:24.330 align:middle line:84%
L1, 1 power of L,
shifts back once.

00:53:24.330 --> 00:53:27.290 align:middle line:84%
If we apply L
twice or L squared,

00:53:27.290 --> 00:53:29.290 align:middle line:90%
this shifts back two periods.

00:53:29.290 --> 00:53:33.510 align:middle line:84%
And of course, if we
apply it n times then

00:53:33.510 --> 00:53:35.990 align:middle line:90%
we shift back n periods.

00:53:35.990 --> 00:53:40.310 align:middle line:84%
And so with these
lag operators, we

00:53:40.310 --> 00:53:44.110 align:middle line:84%
can think of inverses of
those operators as well

00:53:44.110 --> 00:53:47.950 align:middle line:84%
and consider shifting
the time series forward

00:53:47.950 --> 00:53:50.950 align:middle line:90%
if that were useful.

00:53:50.950 --> 00:53:58.270 align:middle line:84%
And so when we think of
the Wold representation,

00:53:58.270 --> 00:54:02.550 align:middle line:84%
we can express it as
the sum of the moving

00:54:02.550 --> 00:54:09.190 align:middle line:84%
average process and the linearly
deterministic process Vt.

00:54:09.190 --> 00:54:15.630 align:middle line:84%
And we can represent the
moving average process

00:54:15.630 --> 00:54:20.940 align:middle line:84%
as a polynomial of the
lag operators, possibly

00:54:20.940 --> 00:54:22.500 align:middle line:90%
of infinite order.

00:54:22.500 --> 00:54:27.090 align:middle line:84%
That is, multiplying
the eta t's.

00:54:27.090 --> 00:54:30.580 align:middle line:90%


00:54:30.580 --> 00:54:38.900 align:middle line:84%
Now, a useful application
of this moving average

00:54:38.900 --> 00:54:42.300 align:middle line:84%
representation is
that if we think

00:54:42.300 --> 00:54:53.260 align:middle line:84%
about how was the value of
the process at time t affected

00:54:53.260 --> 00:54:59.980 align:middle line:84%
by the white noise
process eta j lags ago,

00:54:59.980 --> 00:55:05.620 align:middle line:84%
then the partial
derivative of Xt

00:55:05.620 --> 00:55:10.980 align:middle line:84%
with respect to that j
lag back white noise term

00:55:10.980 --> 00:55:13.060 align:middle line:90%
is given by psi j.

00:55:13.060 --> 00:55:16.380 align:middle line:84%
So when psi j is
large in magnitude,

00:55:16.380 --> 00:55:20.410 align:middle line:84%
that means that the
current value of X

00:55:20.410 --> 00:55:24.850 align:middle line:84%
is dependent on the j-th
lag of the white noise.

00:55:24.850 --> 00:55:34.410 align:middle line:84%
And with a representation of the
Xt series by this psi function,

00:55:34.410 --> 00:55:38.960 align:middle line:84%
if one graphs the psi
function over lags.

00:55:38.960 --> 00:55:42.050 align:middle line:90%


00:55:42.050 --> 00:55:44.770 align:middle line:90%
So let me do it over here.

00:55:44.770 --> 00:55:51.140 align:middle line:84%
So if we look at psi j as
a function of j and have--

00:55:51.140 --> 00:55:54.690 align:middle line:90%


00:55:54.690 --> 00:56:01.450 align:middle line:84%
we might have weights psi j
that vary positive and negative.

00:56:01.450 --> 00:56:08.810 align:middle line:84%
And so if this were the case,
it would indicate in this graph

00:56:08.810 --> 00:56:14.530 align:middle line:84%
that the impact
of the white noise

00:56:14.530 --> 00:56:21.330 align:middle line:84%
three periods ago has a
significant impact on X now.

00:56:21.330 --> 00:56:28.810 align:middle line:84%
So it can capture delayed
responses of the time series Xt

00:56:28.810 --> 00:56:32.170 align:middle line:90%
to the white noise terms.

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:41.290 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at the long
range impact of eta j--

00:56:41.290 --> 00:56:49.890 align:middle line:84%
or sorry, the long
range impact of the--

00:56:49.890 --> 00:56:52.170 align:middle line:84%
or the cumulative
response of Xt,

00:56:52.170 --> 00:57:03.370 align:middle line:84%
basically, corresponds
to the sum of the size.

00:57:03.370 --> 00:57:06.650 align:middle line:84%
And so there's basically a
long run cumulative response

00:57:06.650 --> 00:57:15.640 align:middle line:90%
of Xt to a given eta t.

00:57:15.640 --> 00:57:18.520 align:middle line:84%
So this is the long
run cumulative response

00:57:18.520 --> 00:57:21.800 align:middle line:90%
to the white noise terms.

00:57:21.800 --> 00:57:31.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's interesting
about the psi of L process.

00:57:31.960 --> 00:57:40.640 align:middle line:84%
So if we have psi of L is
equal to the sum from 0

00:57:40.640 --> 00:57:47.880 align:middle line:84%
to infinity of
psi j, L to the j,

00:57:47.880 --> 00:57:53.920 align:middle line:84%
it's possible that this
operator is invertible.

00:57:53.920 --> 00:58:03.320 align:middle line:84%
And if it's invertible, there
exists an inverse operator

00:58:03.320 --> 00:58:08.120 align:middle line:84%
with coefficients psi
i star, and the product

00:58:08.120 --> 00:58:14.830 align:middle line:84%
of these two polynomials is
simply equal to the identity.

00:58:14.830 --> 00:58:24.430 align:middle line:84%
And if that's the case that an
inverse of the psi of L operator

00:58:24.430 --> 00:58:35.350 align:middle line:84%
exists, then we can write our
model for the stationary process

00:58:35.350 --> 00:58:40.110 align:middle line:84%
as being equivalent to
applying the inverse operator

00:58:40.110 --> 00:58:41.750 align:middle line:90%
to both sides.

00:58:41.750 --> 00:58:46.150 align:middle line:84%
And so we get an
inverse operator, which

00:58:46.150 --> 00:58:51.650 align:middle line:84%
is a polynomial lags operating
on Xt is equal to the eta t.

00:58:51.650 --> 00:58:57.750 align:middle line:84%
So this corresponds to actually
an autoregression model

00:58:57.750 --> 00:59:01.870 align:middle line:90%
that has white noise terms.

00:59:01.870 --> 00:59:07.430 align:middle line:84%
And so when this exists,
this is the format of that.

00:59:07.430 --> 00:59:12.420 align:middle line:84%
And what we'd like
to do is show you

00:59:12.420 --> 00:59:15.460 align:middle line:84%
how this works with
some different examples.

00:59:15.460 --> 00:59:17.880 align:middle line:84%
Right now it's perhaps
a bit abstract,

00:59:17.880 --> 00:59:22.060 align:middle line:84%
but when we look at the special
cases of an autoregressive order

00:59:22.060 --> 00:59:26.700 align:middle line:84%
one model, many of these
terms will become clear.

00:59:26.700 --> 00:59:35.020 align:middle line:84%
Now, we can introduce with this
notation of lags and polynomial

00:59:35.020 --> 00:59:42.420 align:middle line:84%
lag operators the
ARMA p,q, models,

00:59:42.420 --> 00:59:47.260 align:middle line:84%
where ARMA stands for
autoregressive and M-A stands

00:59:47.260 --> 00:59:52.860 align:middle line:84%
for moving average models, and
the p corresponds to the order

00:59:52.860 --> 00:59:56.900 align:middle line:84%
of the autoregressive, and
the q corresponds to the order

00:59:56.900 --> 00:59:58.700 align:middle line:90%
of the moving average.

00:59:58.700 --> 01:00:01.540 align:middle line:84%
We can think of
our time series Xt

01:00:01.540 --> 01:00:04.980 align:middle line:84%
being a sum of terms,
autoregressive terms

01:00:04.980 --> 01:00:09.180 align:middle line:84%
on the top row, and
then the second row

01:00:09.180 --> 01:00:15.660 align:middle line:84%
corresponds to moving average
terms of the white noise.

01:00:15.660 --> 01:00:19.630 align:middle line:84%
So notice in the
top row, we have Xt.

01:00:19.630 --> 01:00:23.100 align:middle line:90%


01:00:23.100 --> 01:00:26.220 align:middle line:84%
And actually, if you
look at that equation,

01:00:26.220 --> 01:00:31.500 align:middle line:84%
if we take Xt minus
mu, this turns out

01:00:31.500 --> 01:00:39.140 align:middle line:84%
to be equal to phi 1 Xt
minus 1 minus mu plus phi 2,

01:00:39.140 --> 01:00:40.340 align:middle line:90%
and so forth.

01:00:40.340 --> 01:00:44.500 align:middle line:84%
So these are lag terms
of the mean adjusted X's.

01:00:44.500 --> 01:00:50.700 align:middle line:84%
And then we have the next row is
the sum of the white noise terms

01:00:50.700 --> 01:00:54.940 align:middle line:84%
at time t, t minus
1 up to t minus q.

01:00:54.940 --> 01:01:02.700 align:middle line:84%
So ARMA p,q models are a
parsimonious case of time series

01:01:02.700 --> 01:01:07.690 align:middle line:84%
models where we just have p
autoregressive parameters,

01:01:07.690 --> 01:01:12.690 align:middle line:84%
phi 1 to phi p, and q
moving average parameters,

01:01:12.690 --> 01:01:14.060 align:middle line:90%
theta 1 to theta q.

01:01:14.060 --> 01:01:21.610 align:middle line:90%


01:01:21.610 --> 01:01:26.730 align:middle line:84%
If we group all the terms
with X's on the left-hand side

01:01:26.730 --> 01:01:30.410 align:middle line:84%
and all the terms with the
etas on the right-hand side,

01:01:30.410 --> 01:01:34.210 align:middle line:84%
we then get this phi
of L operator times

01:01:34.210 --> 01:01:38.970 align:middle line:84%
Xt mu view is equal
to theta of L eta t.

01:01:38.970 --> 01:01:47.450 align:middle line:84%
And the Wold decomposition
of this ARMA model

01:01:47.450 --> 01:01:53.570 align:middle line:84%
is to multiply this ARMA
equation on both sides

01:01:53.570 --> 01:02:02.210 align:middle line:84%
by the inverse of phi of L.
And so mu plus psi of L eta t

01:02:02.210 --> 01:02:11.400 align:middle line:84%
is the Wold decomposition
representation of the process.

01:02:11.400 --> 01:02:19.000 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at
autoregressive models.

01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:23.000 align:middle line:84%
So we'll assume that there's
no moving average term.

01:02:23.000 --> 01:02:30.840 align:middle line:84%
So we have the phi of L operator
operating on Xt minus mu

01:02:30.840 --> 01:02:34.240 align:middle line:90%
is equal to the white noise.

01:02:34.240 --> 01:02:40.520 align:middle line:84%
And so that model
basically says that Xt

01:02:40.520 --> 01:02:49.480 align:middle line:84%
is a linear combination of the
p lags of Xt plus white noise.

01:02:49.480 --> 01:02:54.440 align:middle line:84%
So this is a regression
model for our time

01:02:54.440 --> 01:02:59.520 align:middle line:84%
series Xt using predictor
variables the lags of this time

01:02:59.520 --> 01:03:00.880 align:middle line:90%
series.

01:03:00.880 --> 01:03:12.430 align:middle line:84%
And if we take expectations of
both sides, we basically get mu.

01:03:12.430 --> 01:03:18.390 align:middle line:84%
The constant expectation of the
covariance stationary process

01:03:18.390 --> 01:03:24.230 align:middle line:84%
is equal to C plus the sum of
phi j times mu, the expectation

01:03:24.230 --> 01:03:27.990 align:middle line:90%
of each of the lags, plus 0.

01:03:27.990 --> 01:03:33.470 align:middle line:84%
And so C turns out to
equal mu times phi of 1.

01:03:33.470 --> 01:03:41.060 align:middle line:84%
So we have that representation
of the constant term.

01:03:41.060 --> 01:03:44.470 align:middle line:90%


01:03:44.470 --> 01:03:51.990 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's important
with time series models

01:03:51.990 --> 01:03:59.910 align:middle line:84%
is whether the time series
model is stationary or not.

01:03:59.910 --> 01:04:07.190 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that for
an autoregressive model with

01:04:07.190 --> 01:04:15.230 align:middle line:84%
parameters phi 1 to phi p, that
if we look at what's called this

01:04:15.230 --> 01:04:24.630 align:middle line:84%
characteristic equation, 1 minus
phi 1z minus phi 2z squared up

01:04:24.630 --> 01:04:28.030 align:middle line:90%
to phi pz to the p power.

01:04:28.030 --> 01:04:35.590 align:middle line:84%
If we consider the roots
of that polynomial,

01:04:35.590 --> 01:04:42.230 align:middle line:84%
then, well, if we have
roots lambda 1 to lambda p,

01:04:42.230 --> 01:04:48.770 align:middle line:84%
then phi of L has to be
the product of these terms.

01:04:48.770 --> 01:04:52.670 align:middle line:84%
Each of these terms,
each of these factors

01:04:52.670 --> 01:05:00.310 align:middle line:84%
are equal to zero when
z equals lambda 1.

01:05:00.310 --> 01:05:03.140 align:middle line:90%


01:05:03.140 --> 01:05:07.920 align:middle line:84%
So if z equals lambda 1,
then the first term is zero.

01:05:07.920 --> 01:05:14.060 align:middle line:84%
If z equals the p-th root,
lambda p, then this last factor

01:05:14.060 --> 01:05:14.760 align:middle line:90%
is zero.

01:05:14.760 --> 01:05:19.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is a representation
of this p-th order polynomial

01:05:19.980 --> 01:05:21.350 align:middle line:90%
in terms of its roots.

01:05:21.350 --> 01:05:24.620 align:middle line:90%


01:05:24.620 --> 01:05:27.420 align:middle line:84%
And the condition
for stationarity

01:05:27.420 --> 01:05:31.700 align:middle line:84%
is that all of these roots
have to lie outside the unit

01:05:31.700 --> 01:05:36.820 align:middle line:90%
circle in the complex plane.

01:05:36.820 --> 01:05:45.100 align:middle line:84%
And so if we look at
one of these roots,

01:05:45.100 --> 01:05:50.380 align:middle line:84%
fix just lambda as
a root, then if we

01:05:50.380 --> 01:05:56.220 align:middle line:84%
take the inverse
of this factor, it

01:05:56.220 --> 01:06:02.290 align:middle line:84%
has this geometric metric
sum expression of 1

01:06:02.290 --> 01:06:05.310 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 over lambda L plus
1 over lambda squared,

01:06:05.310 --> 01:06:07.650 align:middle line:90%
L squared, and so forth.

01:06:07.650 --> 01:06:13.370 align:middle line:84%
And so it's basically an
infinite order polynomial

01:06:13.370 --> 01:06:17.250 align:middle line:90%
in lags.

01:06:17.250 --> 01:06:22.690 align:middle line:84%
And the coefficients are 1
over lambda to the I th power.

01:06:22.690 --> 01:06:30.090 align:middle line:84%
So if these lambdas are
bigger than 1 inch magnitude,

01:06:30.090 --> 01:06:36.450 align:middle line:84%
then the inverse of those,
the reciprocal of those,

01:06:36.450 --> 01:06:40.090 align:middle line:90%
is smaller than 1 in magnitude.

01:06:40.090 --> 01:06:47.450 align:middle line:84%
So these terms will die down
with a limiting value of zero.

01:06:47.450 --> 01:06:52.490 align:middle line:84%
And that's necessary
for this representation

01:06:52.490 --> 01:06:56.640 align:middle line:90%
to be appropriate.

01:06:56.640 --> 01:07:01.160 align:middle line:84%
So we get a covariance
stationary sum

01:07:01.160 --> 01:07:05.880 align:middle line:84%
of lagged errors,
lagged white noise

01:07:05.880 --> 01:07:09.780 align:middle line:90%
terms with this representation.

01:07:09.780 --> 01:07:14.840 align:middle line:84%
So the phi inverse of L
is actually the product

01:07:14.840 --> 01:07:21.120 align:middle line:90%
of p of these reciprocals.

01:07:21.120 --> 01:07:25.920 align:middle line:84%
And so the condition that
each of those reciprocals

01:07:25.920 --> 01:07:32.120 align:middle line:84%
or each of those terms
is defined by lambda j's

01:07:32.120 --> 01:07:35.440 align:middle line:84%
that are bigger
than 1 in magnitude

01:07:35.440 --> 01:07:40.880 align:middle line:84%
makes their geometric
series expression converge.

01:07:40.880 --> 01:07:46.720 align:middle line:84%
If the lambdas were inside the
unit circle, the magnitude,

01:07:46.720 --> 01:07:50.860 align:middle line:84%
then when we multiply them
together, square them,

01:07:50.860 --> 01:07:53.640 align:middle line:90%
they get bigger and bigger.

01:07:53.640 --> 01:07:56.105 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:07:56.105 --> 01:08:00.200 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a look
at what this works out

01:08:00.200 --> 01:08:03.720 align:middle line:84%
to be, just for an
autoregressive process with p

01:08:03.720 --> 01:08:05.240 align:middle line:90%
equal to 1.

01:08:05.240 --> 01:08:08.640 align:middle line:84%
With p equal to 1, our
characteristic equation

01:08:08.640 --> 01:08:12.560 align:middle line:84%
is simply 1 minus
phi z equals 0.

01:08:12.560 --> 01:08:19.399 align:middle line:84%
And so the root of that
is clearly 1 over phi.

01:08:19.399 --> 01:08:24.479 align:middle line:84%
And so the p equals 1
autoregressive model

01:08:24.479 --> 01:08:26.800 align:middle line:84%
is going to be covariance
stationary if and only

01:08:26.800 --> 01:08:30.880 align:middle line:84%
if the magnitude of phi
is smaller than one,

01:08:30.880 --> 01:08:36.359 align:middle line:84%
or equivalently, its inverse
magnitude is greater than 1.

01:08:36.359 --> 01:08:43.120 align:middle line:84%
And so we end up having these
first and second moments

01:08:43.120 --> 01:08:49.240 align:middle line:84%
and these covariances which
are very easy to compute.

01:08:49.240 --> 01:08:54.370 align:middle line:84%
And this will probably be a
homework exercise problem.

01:08:54.370 --> 01:08:57.670 align:middle line:84%
But with the first order
autoregression model,

01:08:57.670 --> 01:09:01.050 align:middle line:84%
we have a constant mean,
a constant variance,

01:09:01.050 --> 01:09:07.069 align:middle line:84%
which is sigma squared over 1
minus phi times sigma squared.

01:09:07.069 --> 01:09:15.470 align:middle line:84%
And the covariances basically
are the variance times phi

01:09:15.470 --> 01:09:17.510 align:middle line:90%
to the j-th power.

01:09:17.510 --> 01:09:25.069 align:middle line:84%
So with an autocorrelation,
the phi is the first order

01:09:25.069 --> 01:09:27.750 align:middle line:84%
autocorrelation,
and phi to the j-th

01:09:27.750 --> 01:09:31.590 align:middle line:84%
is the j-th order
autocorrelation.

01:09:31.590 --> 01:09:32.576 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:09:32.576 --> 01:09:42.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, if phi is between
minus 1 and 1--

01:09:42.670 --> 01:09:46.870 align:middle line:84%
for phi between 0
and 1, the process

01:09:46.870 --> 01:09:50.340 align:middle line:84%
exhibits exponential
mean reversion.

01:09:50.340 --> 01:09:57.080 align:middle line:84%
And when phi is negative but
no larger than negative 1

01:09:57.080 --> 01:10:02.180 align:middle line:84%
in magnitude, then it exhibits
some oscillating exponential

01:10:02.180 --> 01:10:04.100 align:middle line:90%
mean reversion.

01:10:04.100 --> 01:10:08.820 align:middle line:84%
But when phi equals 1,
then the Wold decomposition

01:10:08.820 --> 01:10:15.980 align:middle line:84%
does not exist and the process
is a simple random walk

01:10:15.980 --> 01:10:18.860 align:middle line:90%
which is non-stationary.

01:10:18.860 --> 01:10:26.580 align:middle line:84%
And when phi is greater than 1,
then the process is explosive.

01:10:26.580 --> 01:10:32.380 align:middle line:84%
So in financial markets, these
first order autoregressive

01:10:32.380 --> 01:10:36.980 align:middle line:84%
models actually are
used quite frequently.

01:10:36.980 --> 01:10:40.180 align:middle line:84%
With interest rates
where there's maybe

01:10:40.180 --> 01:10:43.420 align:middle line:84%
a constant long term
interest rate level

01:10:43.420 --> 01:10:45.740 align:middle line:84%
and there's
variation about that,

01:10:45.740 --> 01:10:50.050 align:middle line:84%
this Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process
corresponds to the first order

01:10:50.050 --> 01:10:51.350 align:middle line:90%
autoregressive process.

01:10:51.350 --> 01:10:54.574 align:middle line:90%


01:10:54.574 --> 01:11:03.050 align:middle line:84%
With these other series, these
are financial or time series

01:11:03.050 --> 01:11:08.530 align:middle line:84%
in financial markets where
the level of the series

01:11:08.530 --> 01:11:11.690 align:middle line:84%
is expected to be
stable over time.

01:11:11.690 --> 01:11:16.170 align:middle line:84%
So perhaps interest rate
spreads would be modeled

01:11:16.170 --> 01:11:19.450 align:middle line:90%
as being stable or stationary.

01:11:19.450 --> 01:11:24.376 align:middle line:84%
Real exchange rates,
perhaps, or valuation ratios.

01:11:24.376 --> 01:11:27.010 align:middle line:90%


01:11:27.010 --> 01:11:31.770 align:middle line:84%
So these models arise in
these application areas.

01:11:31.770 --> 01:11:40.450 align:middle line:84%
Now to fit an
autoregressive process,

01:11:40.450 --> 01:11:46.090 align:middle line:84%
we could simply just fit
a linear regression model

01:11:46.090 --> 01:11:51.690 align:middle line:84%
with p lags of the series
as explanatory variables.

01:11:51.690 --> 01:11:56.090 align:middle line:84%
But what's rather
interesting to know

01:11:56.090 --> 01:12:03.850 align:middle line:84%
is this approach called
solving Yule-Walker equations.

01:12:03.850 --> 01:12:10.630 align:middle line:84%
If we take our model equation,
that first equation line,

01:12:10.630 --> 01:12:16.250 align:middle line:84%
we have the mean
deviation of Xt is

01:12:16.250 --> 01:12:20.050 align:middle line:84%
equal to a linear
combination of phi j times

01:12:20.050 --> 01:12:24.610 align:middle line:90%
the j-th lag deviation from mu.

01:12:24.610 --> 01:12:28.690 align:middle line:84%
There's a typo there,
that second phi 2

01:12:28.690 --> 01:12:32.010 align:middle line:90%
should have an Xt minus 2.

01:12:32.010 --> 01:12:37.490 align:middle line:84%
Then if we multiply both
sides of this model equation

01:12:37.490 --> 01:12:44.050 align:middle line:84%
by Xt minus j minus mu, then
we basically, for each term,

01:12:44.050 --> 01:12:49.160 align:middle line:84%
we're adding Xt minus
j minus mu as a factor,

01:12:49.160 --> 01:12:52.120 align:middle line:84%
and then take
expectations of that.

01:12:52.120 --> 01:12:59.320 align:middle line:84%
We have our j-th
lag autocovariance,

01:12:59.320 --> 01:13:05.560 align:middle line:84%
and it's equal to the
phi 1 times the j minus 1

01:13:05.560 --> 01:13:10.880 align:middle line:84%
lag autocovariance plus phi
2 times the j minus 2 lag,

01:13:10.880 --> 01:13:12.800 align:middle line:90%
and so forth.

01:13:12.800 --> 01:13:20.800 align:middle line:84%
And if the j is equal
to zero, then we also

01:13:20.800 --> 01:13:28.920 align:middle line:84%
have a sigma squared term where
we're looking at basically that.

01:13:28.920 --> 01:13:31.160 align:middle line:90%
Well, that term comes in.

01:13:31.160 --> 01:13:40.720 align:middle line:84%
So we basically have equations,
a system of equations

01:13:40.720 --> 01:13:43.200 align:middle line:90%
if we vary j.

01:13:43.200 --> 01:13:46.090 align:middle line:84%
And so if we consider
j equal to 1 to p,

01:13:46.090 --> 01:13:50.950 align:middle line:84%
we get a system of p linear
equations in the phi j's.

01:13:50.950 --> 01:13:55.470 align:middle line:84%
And this is our
Yule-Walker equations.

01:13:55.470 --> 01:13:59.070 align:middle line:84%
Gamma 1 is equal
to the first row

01:13:59.070 --> 01:14:04.270 align:middle line:84%
of the matrix times the
vector of phis, and so forth.

01:14:04.270 --> 01:14:08.750 align:middle line:84%
And with the properties
of the autocovariances,

01:14:08.750 --> 01:14:11.030 align:middle line:90%
they are symmetrical.

01:14:11.030 --> 01:14:17.870 align:middle line:84%
So we can actually use these
to make the computations

01:14:17.870 --> 01:14:19.790 align:middle line:90%
of the matrices.

01:14:19.790 --> 01:14:24.270 align:middle line:84%
And when we use this
Yule-Walker approach

01:14:24.270 --> 01:14:27.190 align:middle line:84%
to estimate the
parameters, we're

01:14:27.190 --> 01:14:31.990 align:middle line:84%
actually using a method
of moments principle

01:14:31.990 --> 01:14:39.150 align:middle line:84%
for estimating the
autoregressive model parameters.

01:14:39.150 --> 01:14:44.740 align:middle line:84%
Method of moments is a principle
in statistical estimation, where

01:14:44.740 --> 01:14:50.060 align:middle line:84%
we basically equate sample
moments and the population

01:14:50.060 --> 01:14:54.660 align:middle line:84%
moments and solve for what
population parameter values

01:14:54.660 --> 01:14:58.340 align:middle line:90%
match the sample moments.

01:14:58.340 --> 01:15:02.180 align:middle line:84%
Now, we can do a
similar analysis

01:15:02.180 --> 01:15:04.580 align:middle line:90%
of moving average models.

01:15:04.580 --> 01:15:09.540 align:middle line:84%
So here, we have a q-th order
moving average model represented

01:15:09.540 --> 01:15:17.820 align:middle line:84%
by a polynomial theta of L of
order q in terms of powers of L.

01:15:17.820 --> 01:15:24.860 align:middle line:84%
And the properties of this
process can be studied.

01:15:24.860 --> 01:15:28.380 align:middle line:84%
And one thing we
might want to do

01:15:28.380 --> 01:15:34.100 align:middle line:84%
is invert this
moving average model

01:15:34.100 --> 01:15:42.980 align:middle line:84%
into an autoregressive model by
basically multiplying the model

01:15:42.980 --> 01:15:45.780 align:middle line:84%
equation on both
sides by the inverse

01:15:45.780 --> 01:15:49.980 align:middle line:90%
of the theta of L operator.

01:15:49.980 --> 01:15:54.220 align:middle line:84%
And so if we do that,
then we basically

01:15:54.220 --> 01:15:59.420 align:middle line:84%
have an autoregressive
representation of the moving

01:15:59.420 --> 01:16:02.540 align:middle line:90%
average order q model.

01:16:02.540 --> 01:16:07.660 align:middle line:84%
And if you calculate simple
second order properties

01:16:07.660 --> 01:16:13.580 align:middle line:84%
of this moving average process,
well, the mean is simply mu.

01:16:13.580 --> 01:16:16.020 align:middle line:84%
The right-hand side
of the equation

01:16:16.020 --> 01:16:21.700 align:middle line:84%
is just weighted sums of
etas which have mean zero.

01:16:21.700 --> 01:16:25.140 align:middle line:84%
And so the expectation
of the left-hand side

01:16:25.140 --> 01:16:26.940 align:middle line:90%
is simply equal to mu.

01:16:26.940 --> 01:16:30.780 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at
the variance of Xt,

01:16:30.780 --> 01:16:36.580 align:middle line:84%
it basically is the variance of
the weighted sum of the etas.

01:16:36.580 --> 01:16:43.050 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out to have this
simple form with covariances

01:16:43.050 --> 01:16:45.570 align:middle line:90%
of the series.

01:16:45.570 --> 01:16:52.010 align:middle line:84%
With moving average processes,
if we look at time increments

01:16:52.010 --> 01:16:58.290 align:middle line:84%
j that are longer
than the q order,

01:16:58.290 --> 01:17:05.910 align:middle line:84%
then there's no overlap of white
noise terms in Xt and Xt plus j,

01:17:05.910 --> 01:17:08.530 align:middle line:84%
and so their covariance
is going to be zero.

01:17:08.530 --> 01:17:13.130 align:middle line:84%
When there is overlap,
then we get this formula.

01:17:13.130 --> 01:17:14.630 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:17:14.630 --> 01:17:22.690 align:middle line:84%
So next, we want
to deal with how

01:17:22.690 --> 01:17:28.070 align:middle line:84%
we can accommodate
non-stationarity in time series.

01:17:28.070 --> 01:17:32.170 align:middle line:84%
So far we've just assumed
examples and models,

01:17:32.170 --> 01:17:35.330 align:middle line:84%
like autoregressive and
moving average models, where

01:17:35.330 --> 01:17:38.960 align:middle line:90%
this series is stationary.

01:17:38.960 --> 01:17:41.960 align:middle line:84%
There will be cases when
our time series is not

01:17:41.960 --> 01:17:49.680 align:middle line:84%
stationary and differencing
the series will generally

01:17:49.680 --> 01:17:54.720 align:middle line:84%
render a series to a
stationary transformation.

01:17:54.720 --> 01:18:03.930 align:middle line:84%
And so if we consider first
order differencing, we have--

01:18:03.930 --> 01:18:06.800 align:middle line:90%


01:18:06.800 --> 01:18:08.280 align:middle line:90%
let's see here.

01:18:08.280 --> 01:18:13.960 align:middle line:84%
If we do delta Yt
equaling 1 minus L Yt

01:18:13.960 --> 01:18:22.080 align:middle line:84%
equals Yt minus L Yt, which
is Yt minus Yt minus 1.

01:18:22.080 --> 01:18:22.620 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:18:22.620 --> 01:18:25.080 align:middle line:90%
This is first order differences.

01:18:25.080 --> 01:18:30.860 align:middle line:84%
If we apply the first
difference of this,

01:18:30.860 --> 01:18:37.630 align:middle line:84%
we end up getting Yt minus
Yt minus 1 minus Yt minus 1

01:18:37.630 --> 01:18:41.150 align:middle line:90%
minus Yt minus 2.

01:18:41.150 --> 01:18:46.870 align:middle line:84%
And this turns out to be equal
to Yt minus 2, Yt minus 1

01:18:46.870 --> 01:18:51.070 align:middle line:90%
plus Yt minus 2.

01:18:51.070 --> 01:18:53.930 align:middle line:84%
And there could be k-th
order differencing,

01:18:53.930 --> 01:18:57.070 align:middle line:84%
but in practice, we'll
only generally use

01:18:57.070 --> 01:18:59.750 align:middle line:84%
first or second
order differencing.

01:18:59.750 --> 01:19:02.870 align:middle line:84%
And what's really neat
about differencing

01:19:02.870 --> 01:19:10.190 align:middle line:84%
is that first order differencing
will eliminate a linear trend

01:19:10.190 --> 01:19:12.230 align:middle line:90%
in the series.

01:19:12.230 --> 01:19:14.490 align:middle line:84%
Basically, if we
have a linear trend,

01:19:14.490 --> 01:19:20.110 align:middle line:84%
then the difference will just
be a constant difference.

01:19:20.110 --> 01:19:24.630 align:middle line:84%
And the second order
difference is appropriate

01:19:24.630 --> 01:19:32.550 align:middle line:84%
if there is a quadratic
trend in the series

01:19:32.550 --> 01:19:34.390 align:middle line:84%
and second order
differencing will

01:19:34.390 --> 01:19:36.790 align:middle line:90%
eliminate that quadratic trend.

01:19:36.790 --> 01:19:39.330 align:middle line:90%
So we'll finish there for today.

01:19:39.330 --> 01:19:45.550 align:middle line:84%
But this issue of modeling
differences of the series

01:19:45.550 --> 01:19:49.130 align:middle line:84%
or second differences of
the series as stationary,

01:19:49.130 --> 01:19:53.190 align:middle line:84%
corresponds to having
time series models that

01:19:53.190 --> 01:19:58.750 align:middle line:84%
are looking at the dynamics
of the slope of the time

01:19:58.750 --> 01:20:03.270 align:middle line:84%
series, which is like
the first derivative

01:20:03.270 --> 01:20:07.070 align:middle line:84%
or the second
derivative, and assuming

01:20:07.070 --> 01:20:14.110 align:middle line:84%
that those are perhaps constant
level and constant variability.

01:20:14.110 --> 01:20:20.310 align:middle line:84%
So those mathematical features
end up being very intuitively

01:20:20.310 --> 01:20:23.150 align:middle line:84%
applied actually
with different cases.

01:20:23.150 --> 01:20:23.790 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:20:23.790 --> 01:20:26.580 align:middle line:90%
We'll finish there for today.

01:20:26.580 --> 01:20:32.000 align:middle line:90%